//! What Kumi can change in Logic Pro (M3, M4): a track's fader, pan and buttons, its name and its colour, tracks added,
//! deleted, moved and put in Track Stacks, markers, the tempo and the Tempo and Signature Lists, a region's notes as
//! Event List rows (or many at once as a MIDI file imported), regions moved, split, resized, looped, renamed and
//! deleted, the cycle range, a plug-in's bypass, and a key command for what no other tool does. Each kind is the
//! bridge's (`crates/logic-kumi/src/changes`), which makes it through one macOS channel and reads it back; what the
//! bridge found no channel for (a marker's name, a plug-in inserted, removed or set, a send) isn't declared, and the
//! bridge refuses it saying why. Logic has no undo block, and its own undo keeps a step for an edit (a rename, a track,
//! a marker, a region, notes, a list's row, a colour, a stack) and none for a header's control, the tempo, a bypass or
//! the cycle: `plan_steps` is false, and Kumi's undo puts every change back as a new write. The kinds Logic keeps no
//! step for carry `skipped_by_daw_undo`, as the host's `outside_undo` names them. Saving is the action `save_project`
//! (the host's `project_save`): a file written, no change and no row.
use super::own_changes::{OwnAction, OwnChangeSet, OwnKind};
use crate::core::contracts::ChangeFamily::*;

const PLAN: &str = "Make changes in Logic Pro in one call, in order: each step is one of your change tools or a step below with its input, and \"@name\" in an input stands for what an earlier step marked as: \"name\" made (a new track, a new marker, the right part of a split, an imported region, a Track Stack's track). A wait step ({\"beats\": 8} or {\"seconds\": 4}) pauses between steps. Logic Pro has no undo block: a step that renames, colours, adds, deletes, moves or stacks a track, makes, moves or deletes a marker, writes, imports or quantizes notes, moves, splits, resizes, loops, renames or deletes a region, makes or changes a tempo or a signature in its lists, or runs a key command Logic Pro keeps a step for, is one step of Logic Pro's own undo (Cmd-Z in Logic Pro takes back the last one only); a fader, pan, mute, solo, arm, tempo, bypass or cycle step is none, since Logic Pro's undo doesn't keep those. A step that adds, deletes, moves or splits a track or a region makes Logic Pro's refs new: name what it made with as and @name, and read again for the rest. It stops at the first step that fails and says what was done. With final: true and every step done, Kumi tells the producer what changed and the answer ends there, with no reply from you: use it when the changes complete the request, even a single change.";
const UNDO: &str = "Undo one of your changes from this session: pass its change id (such as c3), or \"last\" for the latest one. Kumi puts back exactly what that change set, as a new write in Logic Pro; it only works while nobody changed the same thing in Logic Pro since, and if so, say what happened (discard: true puts it back anyway, only when the producer asks). A deleted track or region and a flattened Track Stack come back only through Logic Pro's own undo, while that step is still its latest; what a key command did only Logic Pro's own undo takes back. With final: true, when the undo is all that was asked, Kumi tells the producer and the answer ends there, with no reply from you.";
const INSTRUCTIONS: &str = "Change Logic Pro with your change tools, or several changes at once with make_changes, using the refs your reads gave. Logic Pro's own undo keeps a step for an edit (a rename, a colour, a track or a marker made, deleted or moved, a Track Stack, a region moved, split, resized, looped, renamed or deleted, notes written, imported or quantized, a tempo or a signature in its lists, a key command that edits) and none for a fader, pan, mute, solo, arm, tempo or bypass, nor for the cycle range (its Edit menu stays as it was); undo_change takes back any change of yours as a new write (Logic Pro's own undo is the producer's), except what a key command did, which only Logic Pro's own undo takes back. Kumi changes Logic Pro through its own window and reads each value back: a change Logic Pro didn't show is answered as uncertain, so read it before going on. Adding, deleting, moving or splitting a track or a region makes Logic Pro's refs new: read again before using older ones (a ref the change made comes back with it). Positions are positionBeats, beats (quarter notes) from the project's start; Logic Pro shows them as bars. A region is what sits on a track in the Tracks area; its startBeats and endBeats count from the project's start, and its notes' start from the region's start. Many notes at once go in with midi_import (Kumi writes a MIDI file and Logic Pro imports it as a new region); notes_add is for a few notes in a region that exists. key_command runs one of Logic Pro's key commands by its English name for what no other tool does, never one that opens a dialog or leaves the project (Open, Save, Save As, Bounce, Import, Export: save_project saves). The cycle range is the locators; a Track Stack gathers tracks under a track of its own. Pan runs -1 (left) to 1 (right) here, though Logic Pro's strips show -64 to 63; the tempo takes whole BPM; Logic Pro names markers itself. The observation's kumiChanges lists your latest changes and where each stands (applied, undone, kept, unsure); the producer can undo one in Logic Pro or in HISTORY without telling you, so go by kumiChanges and Logic Pro, not by memory.";

pub static CHANGES: OwnChangeSet = OwnChangeSet {
    kinds: &KINDS,
    actions: &ACTIONS,
    plan_description: PLAN,
    undo_description: UNDO,
    instructions: INSTRUCTIONS,
    plan_steps: false,
    most_track_adds: None,
};

static ACTIONS: [OwnAction; 4] = [
    OwnAction { tool: "play", method: "transport_play", description: "Start Logic Pro playing from the playhead.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"properties":{}}"#, core: true, playing: Some(true), title: "Playing" },
    OwnAction { tool: "stop", method: "transport_stop", description: "Stop Logic Pro's playback and recording.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"properties":{}}"#, core: true, playing: Some(false), title: "Stopped" },
    OwnAction { tool: "set_cursor", method: "cursor_set", description: "Move Logic Pro's playhead to positionBeats (beats from the project's start, to the bar and beat) or position (seconds at the project's tempo); playback follows while it plays.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"positionBeats":{"type":"number","minimum":0},"position":{"type":"number","minimum":0}}}"#, core: false, playing: None, title: "Moved the playhead" },
    // (M4) Saving writes the package and changes nothing in the project: no row in HISTORY. Logic keeps no versions (that
    // is REAPER's), so the host's `version` isn't offered.
    OwnAction { tool: "save_project", method: "project_save", description: "Save the Logic Pro project where it is (File > Save), or as path (a whole path to a .logicx package that isn't there yet; the project is that file from then on). A project never saved is saved only under path, and never over a file. No row in HISTORY: saving changes nothing in the project.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":4096}}}"#, core: false, playing: None, title: "Saved the project" },
];

